2.7 Missing values inside groups
Missing values behave the same as in last session, one group at a time.

movies |&gt;
  summarize(avg_runtime = mean(runtime), .by = genre) |&gt;
  filter(is.na(avg_runtime))

#&gt; # A tibble: 1 x 2
#&gt;   genre       avg_runtime
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;             &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 Documentary          NA


One documentary has no runtime, so the whole Documentary average is NA. Here are two honest options (a third is in the box below):

# 1. Ignore missing values, and say so
movies |&gt;
  summarize(
    avg_runtime = mean(runtime, na.rm = TRUE),
    n_missing = sum(is.na(runtime)),
    .by = genre
  ) |&gt;
  filter(genre == "Documentary")

#&gt; # A tibble: 1 x 3
#&gt;   genre       avg_runtime n_missing
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;             &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
#&gt; 1 Documentary        96.5         1

# 2. Remove those rows first
movies |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(runtime)) |&gt;
  summarize(avg_runtime = mean(runtime), .by = genre) |&gt;
  filter(genre == "Documentary")

#&gt; # A tibble: 1 x 2
#&gt;   genre       avg_runtime
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;             &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 Documentary        96.5








na.rm = TRUE is a decision, not a fix



Always report how many values were dropped (n_missing above). A third option is to not summarize at all until you understand why the value is missing.

2.9 mutate() per group: keep every row
summarize() shrinks the table. mutate() with groups keeps every row and adds a column computed per group. This is how you compare each row to its own group.

movies |&gt;
       mutate(
              genre_avg = mean(audience_score),
              .by = genre,
              vs_genre = audience_score - genre_avg
       ) |&gt;
       select(title, genre, audience_score, genre_avg, vs_genre) |&gt;
       arrange(desc(vs_genre)) |&gt;
       head(5)

#&gt; # A tibble: 5 x 5
#&gt;   title                  genre              audience_score genre_avg vs_genre
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;                  &lt;chr&gt;                       &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 The Godfather, Part II Mystery & Suspense             97      55.9     41.1
#&gt; 2 Annie Hall             Comedy                         93      52.5     40.5
#&gt; 3 Aliens                 Action & Adventure             94      53.8     40.2
#&gt; 4 Waiting for Guffman    Comedy                         91      52.5     38.5
#&gt; 5 Aladdin                Action & Adventure             92      53.8     38.2


A common use is a share within a group:

movies |&gt;
  count(genre, mpaa_rating) |&gt;
  mutate(share = n / sum(n), .by = genre) |&gt;
  filter(genre == "Horror")

#&gt; # A tibble: 3 x 4
#&gt;   genre  mpaa_rating     n  share
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;  &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;int&gt;  &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 Horror PG              2 0.0870
#&gt; 2 Horror R              19 0.826 
#&gt; 3 Horror Unrated         2 0.0870

3.4 Keys with different names, and more than one key
When the key columns have different names, say so with join_by():

studios_renamed &lt;- studios |&gt; rename(studio_name = studio)

movies |&gt;
  left_join(studios_renamed, by = join_by(studio == studio_name)) |&gt;
  nrow()

#&gt; [1] 651


When the match needs two or more columns, list them all. A useful pattern is to summarize, then join the summary back to compare each row to a benchmark:

benchmark &lt;- movies |&gt;
  summarize(bench_score = mean(audience_score), .by = c(genre, mpaa_rating))

movies |&gt;
  left_join(benchmark, by = c("genre", "mpaa_rating")) |&gt;
  mutate(vs_bench = audience_score - bench_score) |&gt;
  select(title, genre, mpaa_rating, audience_score, bench_score, vs_bench) |&gt;
  head(4)

#&gt; # A tibble: 4 x 6
#&gt;   title                genre  mpaa_rating audience_score bench_score vs_bench
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;                &lt;chr&gt;  &lt;chr&gt;                &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 Filly Brown          Drama  R                       73        65.1     7.89
#&gt; 2 The Dish             Drama  PG-13                   81        63.8    17.2 
#&gt; 3 Waiting for Guffman  Comedy R                       91        58.7    32.3 
#&gt; 4 The Age of Innocence Drama  PG                      76        68.1     7.91








Audit it: same-named columns get .x and .y



If both tables have a non-key column with the same name, dplyr keeps both and adds suffixes.

studios_with_genre &lt;- studios |&gt; mutate(genre = "lookup genre")

movies |&gt;
  left_join(studios_with_genre, by = "studio") |&gt;
  select(title, starts_with("genre")) |&gt;
  head(3)

#&gt; # A tibble: 3 x 3
#&gt;   title               genre.x genre.y     
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;               &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;       
#&gt; 1 Filly Brown         Drama   &lt;NA&gt;        
#&gt; 2 The Dish            Drama   lookup genre
#&gt; 3 Waiting for Guffman Comedy  lookup genre


Code written later that refers to genre will fail or, worse, pick up the wrong column. Join on every column that means the same thing, or rename one of them first.

4.2 pivot_wider(): rows into columns
Use pivot_wider() to build a cross-table for reading, or to compare columns side by side.

movies |&gt;
  count(title_type, mpaa_rating) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = mpaa_rating, values_from = n, values_fill = 0)

#&gt; # A tibble: 3 x 7
#&gt;   title_type       G    PG `PG-13`     R Unrated `NC-17`
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt;   &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt;   &lt;int&gt;   &lt;int&gt;
#&gt; 1 Documentary      3     8       3     9      32       0
#&gt; 2 Feature Film    16   110     130   317      16       2
#&gt; 3 TV Movie         0     0       0     3       2       0


names_from supplies the new column names and values_from supplies the cells. Combinations that never occur would be NA. values_fill = 0 replaces them with 0, which is correct for counts and misleading for averages.

movies |&gt;
  pivot_longer(
    c(critics_score, audience_score),
    names_to = "source",
    values_to = "score"
  ) |&gt;
  summarize(avg = mean(score), .by = c(genre, source)) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = source, values_from = avg) |&gt;
  mutate(gap = critics_score - audience_score) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(gap)) |&gt;
  head(5)

#&gt; # A tibble: 5 x 4
#&gt;   genre                     critics_score audience_score    gap
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;                             &lt;dbl&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;  &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 Documentary                        86.3           82.8  3.60 
#&gt; 2 Science Fiction & Fantasy          50             50.9 -0.889
#&gt; 3 Mystery & Suspense                 54.9           55.9 -1.02 
#&gt; 4 Other                              64.9           66.7 -1.81 
#&gt; 5 Horror                             44.0           45.8 -1.87


Where do critics and audiences disagree most? A long-to-wide pivot makes the gap one subtraction.






Audit it: pivot_wider() on rows that are not unique



pivot_wider() expects each combination of the other columns plus names_from to appear once. If not, you get list-columns instead of numbers.

tibble(id = c(1, 1, 2), k = c("a", "a", "a"), v = c(10, 20, 30)) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = k, values_from = v)

#&gt; Warning: Values from `v` are not uniquely identified; output will contain list-cols.
#&gt; * Use `values_fn = list` to suppress this warning.
#&gt; * Use `values_fn = {summary_fun}` to summarise duplicates.
#&gt; * Use the following dplyr code to identify duplicates.
#&gt;   {data} |&gt;
#&gt;   dplyr::summarise(n = dplyr::n(), .by = c(id, k)) |&gt;
#&gt;   dplyr::filter(n &gt; 1L)


#&gt; # A tibble: 2 x 2
#&gt;      id a        
#&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;list&gt;   
#&gt; 1     1 &lt;dbl [2]&gt;
#&gt; 2     2 &lt;dbl [1]&gt;


A column of &lt;dbl [2]&gt; is a sign that rows were duplicated. Summarize first (for example, take the mean() per id and k), or fix the duplicate rows.








Audit a pivot by counting rows



After pivot_longer(), rows = old rows x number of columns pivoted:

nrow(scores)

#&gt; [1] 651

nrow(scores_long)

#&gt; [1] 1302


651 x 2 = 1,302. After pivot_wider(), check for new NAs and for list-columns.

5.5 The same verbs from Section 2 and 3 work here

views |&gt;
  filter(completed) |&gt;
  group_by(country) |&gt;
  summarize(n = n(), avg_min = mean(minutes)) |&gt;
  collect() |&gt;
  arrange(desc(n))

#&gt; # A tibble: 8 x 3
#&gt;   country      n avg_min
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;int&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 US      199841    107.
#&gt; 2 IN       63471    107.
#&gt; 3 UK       63450    107.
#&gt; 4 BR       52947    107.
#&gt; 5 DE       47760    107.
#&gt; # i 3 more rows
Counting distinct values and working with dates also happen inside arrow. Here we count users and titles, and views per month:

views |&gt;
  summarize(
    views = n(),
    users = n_distinct(user_id),
    titles = n_distinct(title)
  ) |&gt;
  collect()

#&gt; # A tibble: 1 x 3
#&gt;     views  users titles
#&gt;     &lt;int&gt;  &lt;int&gt;  &lt;int&gt;
#&gt; 1 4000000 250000    646

views |&gt;
  mutate(month = month(view_date)) |&gt;
  count(month) |&gt;
  collect() |&gt;
  arrange(month)

#&gt; # A tibble: 12 x 2
#&gt;    month      n
#&gt;    &lt;int&gt;  &lt;int&gt;
#&gt;  1     1 338884
#&gt;  2     2 316616
#&gt;  3     3 338816
#&gt;  4     4 327417
#&gt;  5     5 337523
#&gt;  6     6 329013
#&gt;  7     7 338883
#&gt;  8     8 339398
#&gt;  9     9 327283
#&gt; 10    10 339938
#&gt; 11    11 328043
#&gt; 12    12 338186
(The type of month may print as &lt;int&gt; or &lt;dbl&gt; depending on your version of arrow; the values are the same.)

11.1 if and else
if tests one TRUE/FALSE. It is not vectorized.

x &lt;- 7
if (x &lt; 0) {
  "negative"
} else if (x &gt; 0) {
  "positive"
} else {
  "zero"
}

#&gt; [1] "positive"


To choose values for a whole column, use the vectorized if_else() or case_when() inside mutate() instead.






Audit it: if with a vector




if (c(TRUE, FALSE)) {
  "yes"
}

#&gt; Error in `if (c(TRUE, FALSE)) ...`:
#&gt; ! the condition has length &gt; 1


R refuses a condition with length greater than one. If you see if applied to a column, expect an error or a misunderstanding.

Case #0: the request

“Show me the 5 best-rated movies on IMDb.”

The AI wrote:

movies |&gt;
  arrange(imdb_rating) |&gt;
  select(title, imdb_rating) |&gt;
  head(5)


With your partner (60 seconds): restate each line in plain English. Does it match the request? Navigator speaks, driver listens.

Three types you’ll meet

class(72)

#&gt; [1] "numeric"

class("Drama")

#&gt; [1] "character"

class(TRUE)

#&gt; [1] "logical"


numeric, character, logical

Same thing, two ways

head(arrange(filter(movies, genre == "Drama"), imdb_rating), 3)


movies |&gt;
  filter(genre == "Drama") |&gt;
  arrange(imdb_rating) |&gt;
  head(3)

You’ll also see %&gt;%, the older tidyverse pipe. It behaves the same for what we do.

In Positron the pipe shortcut is Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+M. A setting decides which pipe it inserts.

Backup: AI answer for Round 2
Use this if no AI is available. Audit it!

movies |&gt;
  group_by(studio) |&gt;
  summarize(n = n()) |&gt;
  arrange(n) |&gt;
  head(1)


Same bug as Case #0 in a new costume: arrange(n) is ascending, so this returns a studio with a single movie. Fix: arrange(desc(n)). Pattern: AI repeats failure modes, so learn the common ones.

