Same idea as check_rules(), with a formatted report.

6.3 Rare is not wrong
After folding, three values occur only once: blüe, the Cyrillic green, and chartreuse. Two of them are errors that a lookup table can fix, and one (chartreuse) is a real color. Rarity alone cannot tell them apart, so flag rare values and look at each one in context. Do not delete them because they are rare.

humans |&gt;
  mutate(favorite = str_to_lower(str_squish(favorite))) |&gt;
  add_count(favorite, name = "n") |&gt;
  mutate(rare = n &lt; 2) |&gt;
  filter(rare) |&gt;
  distinct(favorite, n)

#&gt; # A tibble: 3 x 2
#&gt;   favorite       n
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;      &lt;int&gt;
#&gt; 1 blüe           1
#&gt; 2 grееn          1
#&gt; 3 chartreuse     1

Replace and tidy: str_replace()

c("  Portland ", "Portland,  OR", "portland") |&gt;
  str_squish() |&gt;
  str_remove(",\\s*OR$") |&gt;
  str_to_title()

#&gt; [1] "Portland" "Portland" "Portland"


str_squish() trims the ends and collapses inner runs of spaces.

Apply the table

humans |&gt;
  mutate(favorite = str_to_lower(str_squish(favorite))) |&gt;
  left_join(fixes, by = c("favorite" = "raw")) |&gt;
  mutate(favorite = coalesce(clean, favorite)) |&gt;
  count(favorite, sort = TRUE)

